{
  "statement": "A cryptographic git commit hash cannot appear in a federal courtroom, absorb civil penalties, or serve prison time when a defective autonomous toy inflicts physical harm on a child. Software version control logs track code authorship, but they possess no legal personhood, hold no corporate assets, and cannot be compelled by judicial subpoena to remediate consumer injuries. Proponents of decentralized build pipelines treat software attribution as a legal substitute for an accountable corporate entity. As documented in the \"AI RMF Playbook\", organizations must explicitly document who is responsible for AI decisions and maintenance, requiring risk management resources proportional to the hazard.\n\nAutomated CI/CD pipelines cannot evaluate the physical material toxicity, structural integrity, or thermal runaway thresholds of a manufactured toy. Software test suites evaluate digital logic gates and execution paths, remaining entirely blind to chemical composition, lead paint migration, or choking hazards embedded in physical polymers. Shifting from legal compliance gates to decentralized code checks replaces tangible safety inspection with unverified algorithmic self-certification. Civil penalties for failing to submit required compliance notices are set at a minimum of $5,000 per instance under the \"Copyright Labeling and Ethical AI Reporting Act (CLEAR Act)\" (2026), proving that statutory frameworks demand human administrative anchors rather than automated build scripts.\n\nDemanding that decentralized pipeline advocates name the specific human legal entity that will pay punitive damages when a signed firmware update causes physical harm exposes the hollowness of automated compliance. Without central compliance audits and named officers bearing statutory liability, corporate actors can shield themselves behind anonymous commit keys and distributed public infrastructure. We spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable, and replacing those legal boundaries with open-source test-suites invites uncontained industrial disaster.",
